Found 2026-08-31 by lane-b060-b062 while verifying B062's removals, and
explicitly flagged as NOT that lane's to chase. Not investigated.

## Observation

On master before B062, `CoordToPos` in tm-editor-plus-plus is reported unused —
but production files call it:

    Editor/WFC.as:472
    Editor/CacheMapBounds.as:7

A function with live call sites should be credited by the ordinary
"referenced anywhere" pass, entirely independently of any export surface.

## Why it is still worth a ticket even though the symptom is gone

B062 silences this warning, but **for the wrong reason** — `CoordToPos` also
has an export stub, so it is now rooted as part of the export surface. The FP
gate is clean either way, which is precisely the problem: **the export rooting
masks a call-site crediting defect rather than fixing it.**

So the latent bug survives for every function that is called but NOT exported.
Nothing currently in the suite would notice.

## Where to look

B061-adjacent. That ticket fixed property-syntax reads not crediting their
`get_`/`set_` accessor, via `record_value_use_variants` and
`qualified_virtual_name`. This is a plain function call not crediting its
callee, which is a different path — but the family is the same: a real
reference that `is_used` cannot match to the declaration, usually because the
recorded spelling and the declared spelling are qualified differently.

Worth checking first whether the call sites are inside a namespace, a class
method, or reached through the namespace ladder — B061 found that `is_used`
deliberately refuses to trust a bare tail as a suffix match, requiring two
`::` segments, and that is exactly the kind of asymmetry that produces this.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)

Red: a global function called from an ordinary production function, in a
fixture where it is NOT exported, asserted silent. Confirm it currently warns.

Variants, per repo CLAUDE.md — sweep the spellings rather than pinning one:

- called from file scope vs from inside `namespace N`
- called as a bare name vs qualified `N::F`
- called from a class method
- called from another namespace entirely
- a genuinely uncalled global still warns (the over-rooting gate)
- an exported-and-called function stays silent for BOTH reasons, so removing
  the export must not resurrect the warning — that is the assertion that would
  have caught this masking

## Related

B061 (property reads not crediting accessors — same family, different path),
B062 (whose export rooting masks this), tm-editor-plus-plus
`Editor/WFC.as`, `Editor/CacheMapBounds.as`.

## ROOT CAUSE + CORPUS MEASUREMENT (lane-unused, 2026-08-31) — premise HELD

**The defect is OVERLOADING.** A called global function is reported unused when
it has more than one overload. `Checker::call_type`'s bare-`Ident` ladder
credits a call through `lookup_function_return`, whose workspace fallback
`lookup_workspace_function_property` returns `None` the moment TWO function
symbols share the name — the deliberate "ambiguous, say nothing" arm. The call
then fell through to the `has_global_ident` silencer, which suppressed the
undefined-identifier diagnostic and recorded **nothing**.

The **namespaced twin of that arm, one screen up, has always called
`record_value_use_variants`.** So the fix is one line restoring a symmetry that
was broken, not a new rule. `src/typecheck/checker.rs:3666`.

An enum used only as a cast-call — `tostring(WheelOrder(w))` — reaches the same
arm, so the fix credits that shape too. Different syntax, same hole.

## The masking claim is CONFIRMED, and the ticket understated it

`CoordToPos` is overloaded 3x AND has an export stub in
`Editor/MacroblockManip_ExportCode.as` (listed in `info.toml` `exports`), so
B062 roots it — exactly as this ticket suspected.

**But a live, visible instance was already in the corpus and nobody had looked
for it.** `LabeledValue` in tm-editor-plus-plus `src/UI/UI_Util.as` — 10
overloads, called from dozens of sites, in no export file — **was being
reported unused ten times on master.** The ticket's belief that the symptom was
entirely masked was a hypothesis, not a fact. Worth remembering: "the symptom
is gone" is a claim about where someone looked.

## Measurement (debug binary, same machine both runs, only checker.rs differing)

| corpus | before | after | delta |
|---|---|---|---|
| tm-editor-plus-plus | 1451 | 1417 | **-34, 0 new** |
| tm-dips-plus-plus | 271 | 251 | **-25, +5 new** |
| tm-archivist | unchanged | | |
| tm-counter | unchanged | | |
| tm-dashboard | unchanged | | |

All 59 removals are overload GROUPS — `LabeledValue` x10, `SmoothLerp`,
`log_debug`, `nvgDrawRect3d` — plus `enum WheelOrder` via the cast-call shape.

## The 5 additions are TRUE positives, and they are the risky half

Verified by hand, and **they were masked by the false positives they sat
behind**:

- `SmoothLerp`'s `t` parameter is genuinely unread (the body uses `g_DT`). It
  was hidden by the no-double-warn gate while the function itself was being
  wrongly reported unused.
- `WheelOrder`'s four values are never named anywhere, and were skipped by the
  documented "a value of an UNUSED enum is skipped" rule — which stopped
  applying once the enum was correctly credited as used.

**These must be pinned as DELIBERATE SCOPE ADDITIONS in labelled tests** (the
B060 precedent). Removing a false positive is the safe direction; ADDING a
diagnostic is the direction that creates one if the reasoning is wrong. The two
halves of a 59-removals/+5-additions result do not carry equal weight and must
not be committed as though they do. The labelling exists so a later lane cannot
silently revert them as false positives.

Note for anyone running a per-diagnostic gate: these 5 appear as ADDITIONS and
will look like a regression unless the gate knows they are intended.

## Red/green

16 tests in a new `called_global_functions_are_credited` module. Red: 9 failed,
7 passed — and **the 7 that passed are exactly the controls**: a non-overloaded
global, the three namespaced arms that already recorded, an overloaded method on
a typed receiver (via `record_member_use_from_type`), and the over-rooting gate
`uncalled_overloads_are_still_reported`. Green: 16/16.

`SUITE-VERIFIED: 2404 passed; 0 failed; 14 ignored; 26/26 binaries reported`
(2388 + 16, exact).